WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:02.840
Film Courage: What do you think makes a great
director?

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:07.730
Bruce Logan, Writer/Director/Cinematographer:
Well for the longest time I was trying to

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:20.080
discern what that was because if you go on
the kind of sets I like where you see everybody

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:27.869
working and you know the AD is shouting out
all of the instructions and doing this and

00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:33.860
that and the whole movie is really taking
place in the director’s head.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:41.750
For me to go on set and see what the director’s
process is is almost impossible because if

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:53.460
he has done his job right and he’s cast
the actors and he has cast the crew and when

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:59.050
he gets to the set except for like steering
the ship (a little bit this way, a little

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:03.400
bit that way) there shouldn’t really be
anything for him to do.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:10.170
He should be able to sit back and let all
of these building blocks that he’s put together

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:14.210
come before that camera that day to let that
happen.

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:19.829
Now there are a lot of directors where that
is not their style.

00:01:19.829 --> 00:01:26.810
Also I always seem to get myself involved
in projects that I don’t have enough time

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:37.829
for and then I have to be very proactive and
Okay the camera is coming here.

00:01:37.829 --> 00:01:40.409
On those kind of movies you can tell who the
real director is.

00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:50.999
But my impression for going on set with some
of the great directors is you can’t really

00:01:50.999 --> 00:01:55.749
see what their process is.

00:01:55.749 --> 00:02:04.590
It’s really hard to know what people’s
process is except for by the quality of their

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:05.590
product.

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:10.929
Film Courage: What do you think of the cliché
that some directors are afraid of actors?

00:02:10.929 --> 00:02:12.470
Is this true?

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:18.129
Or do you think some people operate better
being removed and letting them do their thing

00:02:18.129 --> 00:02:20.760
giving the appearance of fear?

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:28.480
Bruce: Well it’s funny because Kubrick has
a reputation of not really someone said I

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:34.400
think that if he was a CGI animator he’d
be able to control every aspect of his movie

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:40.030
because you are doing it all pixel by pixel.

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:45.319
I think some of his best movie performances
that he got were from an actor called Peter

00:02:45.319 --> 00:02:53.049
Sellers and I’m sure he was an actor that
you could just like wind up and start shooting

00:02:53.049 --> 00:02:59.450
and the performance is there.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:07.950
I think a lot of inexperienced directors are
afraid of the star power of the actors that

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:13.970
they are working with so they don’t quite
know how to deal with that.

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:19.590
And I think a lot of star power actors working
with those directors are saying I’m not

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:25.489
really getting enough from you as a director
so I want to be directed.

00:03:25.489 --> 00:03:31.040
I think there is a lot of layers going on
there you know?

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:40.989
But if you are afraid of your actors that
is not a helpful place to be because those

00:03:40.989 --> 00:03:45.989
actors are your movie.

00:03:45.989 --> 00:03:50.640
I don’t like to give actors direction.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.400
I like that I’ve cast them.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.390
I’ve seen them in the thing.

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:58.310
I sit back and I want to see their performance.

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:05.650
But if there is something that is not working
for me story wise but I’ll never sit back

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:11.200
in the chair and yell at the actor “Hey,
why don’t you do it this way?”

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.880
For me direction is all I will get up out
of my chair.

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:19.209
I will go onto set, I’ll take the actor
to one side and we will have a very personal

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:26.150
conversation about it so actors are your movie.

